@startuml ota-uart-rx
' How a frame from the update agent reaches the state machine, and how the
' answer goes back. Grounded in Src/uart.c (USART2_IRQHandler, uart_read_byte),
' Src/byte_ring.c, Src/main.c (ota_poll), Src/ota_port.c and Src/flash.c.

title One INFO_REQ, from the wire to the answer

skinparam shadowing false

participant "Host agent\n(industrial-hmi)" as host
participant "USART2\n(one byte deep)" as usart
participant "USART2_IRQHandler" as isr
participant "byte_ring\n(512 bytes)" as ring
participant "main loop\nota_poll()" as mainloop
participant "ota_frame_parser" as parser
participant "ota_update" as sm
participant "ota_port\n(composition)" as port

host -> usart : A5 01 01 00 00 00 E9 CD\none byte every 87 us
loop usart gets each byte
  usart -> isr : RXNE
  isr -> usart : read RDR (clears RXNE)
  isr -> ring : push(byte)
end
note right of isr
  Why an interrupt: the USART holds ONE byte,
  and the next overwrites it 87 us later. The
  loop can be busy for 2.7 ms sending a
  telemetry line, so it cannot promise that.
  An overrun raises this same interrupt and is
  cleared through ICR, or it would fire forever.
end note

loop whenever it gets there
  mainloop -> ring : pop()
  ring --> mainloop : byte
  mainloop -> parser : feed(byte)
end
parser -> sm : on_frame(INFO_REQ, SEQ 1, CRC OK)
sm -> port : running()
note right of port
  version 1, bank from flash_running_bank()
  (SYSCFG FB_MODE), sent with uart_send_bytes(),
  CONFIRMED (no trial exists before piece 6)
end note
sm -> port : send(INFO frame)
port -> host : A5 81 01 00 06 00 01 00 00 00 01 00 F3 1C

legend bottom
  One writer (the interrupt) and one reader (the loop), so the ring needs no
  interrupt masking: each side writes only its own index. Full drops the NEW
  byte; the frame CRC catches the gap and the host resends.
  Erase, program, bank switch and confirm answer FAILED until pieces 5 to 7,
  so BEGIN gets NAK FLASH_ERROR: refused honestly, never faked.
endlegend

@enduml
